  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Simulate an inference serving dynamic batcher that groups arriving requests into batches constrained by max batch size and latency timeout.\n\nDynamic batching amortizes memory bandwidth costs over parallel requests, maximizing GPU tensor core utilization.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nB_t = \\{ r_i \\mid t_i - t_{\\text{first}} \\le \\tau_{\\text{wait}} \\land |B_t| \\le B_{\\max} \\}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Track arrival timestamps and sequence lengths for incoming requests\n2. Group requests into batches up to max_batch_size within max_wait_ms window\n3. Dispatch batch when capacity is reached or timeout triggers, reporting padded batch dimensions\n\n### Returns\nList of dispatched batch metadata dictionaries.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Implement Dynamic Request Batching\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nRunning large AI models in production requires massive computing power. During text generation, memory bandwidth (fetching weights and past key-value caches from VRAM) is often the primary bottleneck rather than raw arithmetic compute. **Implement Dynamic Request Batching** optimizes hardware efficiency, memory consumption, or request serving throughput.\n\nDynamic batching amortizes memory bandwidth costs over parallel requests, maximizing GPU tensor core utilization. In this problem, the goal is to: simulate an inference serving dynamic batcher that groups arriving requests into batches constrained by max batch size and latency timeout.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nB_t = \\{ r_i \\mid t_i - t_{\\text{first}} \\le \\tau_{\\text{wait}} \\land |B_t| \\le B_{\\max} \\}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Track arrival timestamps and sequence lengths for incoming requests\n2. **Phase 2**: Group requests into batches up to max_batch_size within max_wait_ms window\n3. **Phase 3**: Dispatch batch when capacity is reached or timeout triggers, reporting padded batch dimensions\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Memory Bandwidth Bottleneck**: Quantizing weights to INT8 or INT4 cuts memory traffic in half or quarters, dramatically increasing token generation speeds.\n* **Serving Systems**: Dynamic batching and PagedAttention maximize GPU utilization by grouping requests arriving at different times.\n* **Latency vs. Throughput**: In production serving, systems engineers balance Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) against overall tokens-per-second throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: requests = [{\"id\": 1, \"arrival_ms\": 1.0, \"prompt_len\": 128}, {\"id\": 2, \"arrival_ms\": 3.0, \"prompt_len\": 256}, {\"id\": 3, \"arrival_ms\": 15.0, \"prompt_len\": 64}], max_batch_size = 2, max_wait_ms = 10.0\n\nOutput: [{\"batch_size\": 2, \"request_ids\": [1, 2], \"dispatch_time\": 11.0, \"max_seq_len\": 256}, {\"batch_size\": 1, \"request_ids\": [3], \"dispatch_time\": 25.0, \"max_seq_len\": 64}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated inference_dynamic_batching under standard test conditions."
